// `val _ = <an expression that does not check>` used to segfault the compiler. The error recovery
// marks a failed declaration poisoned, to suppress the follow-on "undefined variable" errors from
// later uses of the name it failed to bind -- and `_` binds nothing, so it wrote `items[-1]`.
//
// Both diagnostics below must appear. The second is the one that matters: with a binding already
// in this scope the old code wrote the poison marker onto *that* binding instead of crashing, and
// a poisoned binding's errors are suppressed, so `takes(keep)` passing an int where a string goes
// was silently swallowed. That is the half with no crash to notice it.
fn takes(s: string) -> int {
    return byte_len(s)
}

fn main() {
    val keep = 1
    val _ = nope
    print(format("{}", takes(keep)))
}
